WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.534
-Our first guest tonight
is a Tony Award-winning

00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:04.070
and Oscar-nominated actor

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.338
you know from films such as
"Tick, Tick...Boom!,"

00:00:06.338 --> 00:00:07.974
"The Eyes of Tammy Faye,"

00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:10.676
and from his role in "Angels
in America" on Broadway.

00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:13.846
He stars in FX's "Under
the Banner of Heaven."

00:00:13.846 --> 00:00:16.716
The first two episodes premiere
tonight exclusively on Hulu.

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:18.184
Let's take a look.

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.753
-Allen has already admitted
that he's lost his faith and --

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:23.689
-Well, if a lack of LDS faith
gets a man convicted,

00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:25.892
a whole lot of people
heading to Draper prison,

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:27.193
me included --
-I'm not saying that.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:29.929
I'm saying, leaving the church,

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:32.599
that would have created
a lot of grief in his family.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:38.971
They would have put a lot
of pressure on him to return,

00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:41.441
to save themselves the
humiliation of a wayward son.

00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:44.677
And the Laffertys, they're kind
of like Utah Kennedys.

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:46.579
-Good thing I voted for Nixon.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:49.615
-Please welcome back to the
show Andrew Garfield, everybody!

00:00:49.615 --> 00:00:51.417
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:57.723 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.723 --> 00:01:03.996 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:03.996 --> 00:01:05.431
Welcome back.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:08.100
-Hey!
-Lovely to have you here.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.737
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Nice to have you here with me.

00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:13.772
Thanks for coming.
-That's very nice of you.

00:01:13.772 --> 00:01:15.008
That's a real power move,

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:16.276
you saying,
"It's nice to have you here."

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:19.245
-Nice to have you guys here
on my "Late Night" show.

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:20.779
-Thank you.
Thank you for joining us

00:01:20.779 --> 00:01:23.249
and letting yourself be joined.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:27.386
-Yes.
-Congratulations on this show.

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:29.722
This is a very famous book
by Jon Krakauer.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:31.023
-Yeah.
-Came out, I guess,

00:01:31.023 --> 00:01:32.725
over a decade ago, probably.
-Yeah.

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:33.960
-Did you read it
when it came out?

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.195
-I did.
I kind of gobbled it up.

00:01:36.195 --> 00:01:39.298
It's an incredible book called
"Under the Banner of Heaven,"

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:40.966
as Seth said, by Jon Krakauer.

00:01:40.966 --> 00:01:44.137
And it's kind of a dissection
of the Mormon faith

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:45.704
and the history of Mormonism

00:01:45.704 --> 00:01:52.178
and connecting it to a modern
kind of awful murder event

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:54.180
that happened
in the '80s in Utah.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.648
This amazing woman,
Brenda Wright Lafferty,

00:01:56.648 --> 00:01:59.719
who had the courage,
as a woman in the Mormon faith,

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:04.156
to question the kind
of patriarchal structure.

00:02:04.156 --> 00:02:08.260
And hopefully, our series is an
honoring of her and her memory

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.296
and the memory of her daughter.

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:13.967
And within it, I play
a fictionalized character,

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:16.836
a detective, Jeb Pyre,
who's also a Mormon

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:18.604
going through
his own crisis of faith

00:02:18.604 --> 00:02:20.974
as he is pursuing
the truth of this case,

00:02:20.974 --> 00:02:25.812
so he's kind of stuck between,
you know, the truth and his,

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:28.213
you know, family life,
his community.

00:02:28.213 --> 00:02:30.550
He could potentially
lose his wife and his kids

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.786
if he starts to really unpick
the foundations of his faith.

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:36.288
It's kind of
an interesting dilemma.

00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:37.823
-I knew you did
a lot of research.

00:02:37.823 --> 00:02:40.759
You talked to a lot of people
in the Mormon faith.

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:42.494
Were not willing to go
full method

00:02:42.494 --> 00:02:43.996
for the Mormonism,
though, correct?

00:02:43.996 --> 00:02:47.066
As far as, uh...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:48.834
-It would require --
-Yeah.

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:50.536
-More than I'm willing
to offer, yeah.

00:02:50.536 --> 00:02:52.538
-'Cause, like, you wouldn't
be able to, like --

00:02:52.538 --> 00:02:55.174
I mean, you'd have to, like --
no coffee, right?

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:56.609
-No coffee.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:59.645
I think I'd have to be married
to get to the celestial kingdom,

00:02:59.645 --> 00:03:02.581
which I am not, you know --
-But the coffee.

00:03:02.581 --> 00:03:04.516
-I know, the coffee.
Yeah, exactly.

00:03:04.516 --> 00:03:06.819
[ Laughter ]
No sugar.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:10.856
It's a really -- You know,
their body is their temple.

00:03:10.856 --> 00:03:13.025
Listen, there are so many things
about the Mormon faith

00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:15.394
that I found really --
like, the sense of community

00:03:15.394 --> 00:03:18.931
and there's a real kind of
kindness with modern Mormons.

00:03:18.931 --> 00:03:20.566
But, yeah, the coffee thing,
you know, no way.

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:22.368
-Yeah.
-There's no way.

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:23.669
-You kind of, like,
stop listening.

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:24.970
They're like,
"it's community faith."

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:26.873
Like, "I'm sorry, I'm hung up
on coffee right now.

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:28.574
It's gonna be a hard time
for me to move on."

00:03:28.574 --> 00:03:31.210
So, last time you were here,
it's a while back now,

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.911
you were talking
about "Silence."

00:03:32.911 --> 00:03:34.681
You played a Jesuit priest
in that.

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:36.248
"Eyes of Tammy Faye,"
televangelist.

00:03:36.248 --> 00:03:37.950
And now this.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.886
Is it a coincidence, or is it
interesting to jump into these?

00:03:40.886 --> 00:03:42.555
'Cause these movies
aren't just about faith,

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:46.559
but obviously there's a huge --
or TV shows, I should say.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:48.961
It is an element to all three.
-Definitely.

00:03:48.961 --> 00:03:51.563
But also, I would even say,
"Angels in America,"

00:03:51.563 --> 00:03:56.168
which you mentioned, is maybe
the most spiritual kind of text

00:03:56.168 --> 00:03:57.637
of all of the things
you've talked about.

00:03:57.637 --> 00:03:59.471
And like, something like
"Tick, Tick...Boom!,"

00:03:59.471 --> 00:04:00.839
like, Jonathan Larson

00:04:00.839 --> 00:04:05.278
was a kind of monk
devotee of musical theater.

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:07.947
So I think there's
a spiritual element

00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:09.649
to everything
that I feel drawn to.

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:11.417
And I think it's really
about people

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:14.787
reckoning with life and death
and the deepest questions.

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:17.356
And I think, weirdly enough,
maybe Jim Bakker is

00:04:17.356 --> 00:04:19.125
the least spiritual person
I've ever played.

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:21.360
There's something kind
of interesting about that.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.262
-Yeah, because
he's just a con man.

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:24.597
-Well, I don't know about that.

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:26.098
I have to defend him
a little bit,

00:04:26.098 --> 00:04:27.800
but he was definitely
kind of seduced

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.269
by the things that
we're all seduced by,

00:04:30.269 --> 00:04:33.172
which is kind of
a sense of our own

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:35.808
not-enough-ness,
which makes ourselves, you know,

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:37.409
feel like we need
to fulfill ourselves

00:04:37.409 --> 00:04:39.345
through money
and power and status

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:40.980
and things that you wouldn't
know anything about.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.117
-No, 'cause, for me,
it's just coffee.

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:47.553
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:50.556
You know what?
I'm a fan of true crime.

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:53.325
I also do love when,
in a project like this,

00:04:53.325 --> 00:04:54.793
it is paired
with something else, right?

00:04:54.793 --> 00:04:56.596
It's not just about a mystery,
like you mentioned.

00:04:56.596 --> 00:04:59.165
Like, it is a crisis of faith,
both of the victim

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:01.533
and, you know, the detective
who's investigating the case.

00:05:01.533 --> 00:05:03.636
Are you a true-crime
fan yourself?

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:05.471
-I do enjoy --

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:08.408
I think, recently, I actually
said this in an interview.

00:05:08.408 --> 00:05:09.708
I don't know why
I'm repeating it again.

00:05:09.708 --> 00:05:11.110
Why am I doing this to myself?

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:14.680
But someone said that, "We heard
you love true crime, Andrew,

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:15.948
and you're an easy
sell on this."

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:17.216
I was like, "Yeah, are you
saying I'm easy, like

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:18.351
I'm a slut for true crime?

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:21.320
Like, I guess I am a slut."

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.255
I'm doubling down.
-You're doubling down, yeah.

00:05:23.255 --> 00:05:25.325
-I'm a slut for true crime.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:29.561
For true crime.
Just for true crime.

00:05:29.561 --> 00:05:32.798
-You know, the problem is,
with digital technology today,

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:34.300
they can just edit
that last part off.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:36.702
[ Laughter ]

00:05:36.702 --> 00:05:38.804
-They can edit my outfit
as well.

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:40.807
-There's a lot of --
You're a floating head saying,

00:05:40.807 --> 00:05:45.244
"I'm a slut,"
and, like, a hard cutout.

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:47.447
-Oh, now someone is
definitely gonna do that.

00:05:47.447 --> 00:05:49.048
-Yeah, no, I really have laid
out a pretty

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:52.151
easy-to-follow road map there.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:55.788
So, you mentioned
"Angels in America."

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:58.924
I saw you in
"Death of a Salesman."

00:05:58.924 --> 00:06:00.927
I think I talked about this
last time you were there.

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:02.395
The night I saw it,
I then went to a bar,

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:04.130
and you were there afterwards.
Maybe I haven't.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.499
And you were talking
to the woman

00:06:06.499 --> 00:06:07.667
who played your mother,
who was wonderful.

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:09.434
-Linda Emond, yeah.
The great Linda Emond.

00:06:09.434 --> 00:06:12.138
-And it was too late.

00:06:12.138 --> 00:06:13.406
I realized it too late.

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:15.641
But it was obviously
such an intense show to perform.

00:06:15.641 --> 00:06:17.109
And you guys were sort of deep
in conversation.

00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:19.378
I just walked over,
I was like, "Hey! Great job!"

00:06:19.378 --> 00:06:22.348
And you were both like, "Uh..."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.348 --> 00:06:24.049
I realized, like,
"Oh, it definitely takes them

00:06:24.049 --> 00:06:26.118
a little bit longer to get out
of performing the show

00:06:26.118 --> 00:06:29.354
than it did for me to,
like, watch, seeing the show."

00:06:29.354 --> 00:06:32.592
And that was Mike Nichols.

00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:34.827
-Directing it, yeah.
-And, you know,

00:06:34.827 --> 00:06:37.930
I just read his biography,
which I loved.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.665
And I was wondering if you could
tell me,

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:42.634
because I only met him once,
obviously, you know,

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:43.870
here was this guy
with an incredible career,

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:45.271
and you would assume,
with the wide shot,

00:06:45.271 --> 00:06:47.106
he was just,
like, living on the high life.

00:06:47.106 --> 00:06:49.708
And yet it was really a book
about how this great artist

00:06:49.708 --> 00:06:51.243
constantly had doubt,

00:06:51.243 --> 00:06:53.378
even though it seemed
like one of his skills

00:06:53.378 --> 00:06:56.582
was taking doubt away from the
performers that he worked with.

00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:58.651
-That's so interesting.
Yeah, I think that's true.

00:06:58.651 --> 00:07:05.157
I think it's so reassuring when
an icon and a legend exposes

00:07:05.157 --> 00:07:07.459
that they are
just as insecure as you are.

00:07:07.459 --> 00:07:11.129
I remember, one of the first --
I have a story

00:07:11.129 --> 00:07:14.233
that just came to me now,
perfect for a late-night show.

00:07:14.233 --> 00:07:17.069
[ Laughter ]
Imagine that.

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:20.138
So, I enter the first week
of rehearsal,

00:07:20.138 --> 00:07:23.209
and I am riddled
with insecurity,

00:07:23.209 --> 00:07:26.011
as one always is when one
is putting on a play

00:07:26.011 --> 00:07:28.814
or making a film or doing
anything human, for that matter.

00:07:28.814 --> 00:07:31.584
Just walking down the street,
riddled with insecurity.

00:07:31.584 --> 00:07:33.052
[ Laughter ]

00:07:33.052 --> 00:07:35.221
And I remember
coming up to Mike,

00:07:35.221 --> 00:07:37.689
and him just kind of looking
at me with this kind of benign,

00:07:37.689 --> 00:07:41.259
just kind of appreciation of me,
and I'm just full of doubt.

00:07:41.259 --> 00:07:44.230
And he says, "You know,
I'm not worried about you

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:47.599
because you really don't think
highly of yourself at all."

00:07:47.599 --> 00:07:49.434
[ Laughter ]
And it was like a kind of

00:07:49.434 --> 00:07:53.205
blessing of the wound
of being a person,

00:07:53.205 --> 00:07:56.542
of being someone who just
struggles, like we all struggle.

00:07:56.542 --> 00:08:00.813
And to hear him say,
"You're just like me,"

00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:03.282
and it was about a woundedness
that I have,

00:08:03.282 --> 00:08:05.284
I was like,
"Oh, my God, you are --

00:08:05.284 --> 00:08:07.185
You should be
everyone's grandfather,

00:08:07.185 --> 00:08:10.323
whether they're in the theater
or in film or otherwise."

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:11.390
And that was another thing
about him,

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:14.560
is that he became
a kind of mentor

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.428
to everybody
that he would meet.

00:08:16.428 --> 00:08:20.700
And I think, like Sondheim
became, you know.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:22.501
And it's just a beautiful thing.

00:08:22.501 --> 00:08:25.070
And the way he directed was,
he would tell stories.

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.639
We would finish a scene
at the table,

00:08:27.639 --> 00:08:31.477
and he would tell some kind of
seemingly inconsequential story

00:08:31.477 --> 00:08:34.713
about a party
where he met Elia Kazan

00:08:34.713 --> 00:08:37.016
and asked him about,
you know, naming names.

00:08:37.016 --> 00:08:38.216
And then he'd be like,
"Okay, try it again."

00:08:38.216 --> 00:08:40.319
And then suddenly you're
doing the scene properly

00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:43.856
because of some aspect
of the story that has,

00:08:43.856 --> 00:08:46.125
like,
affected your unconscious.

00:08:46.125 --> 00:08:48.961
And suddenly you're in the scene
in a way that -- I don't know.

00:08:48.961 --> 00:08:54.266
He was a magician and elegant
and yet deeply, deeply insecure.

00:08:54.266 --> 00:08:56.535
-Yeah, I met him.
Like I said, I met him once.

00:08:56.535 --> 00:08:58.770
And yet it was a very short
interaction,

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:00.806
where I felt like he,

00:09:00.806 --> 00:09:02.708
like, managed to transfer
a little bit of light.

00:09:02.708 --> 00:09:05.210
And I thought, "Wow, you
probably knew, for the whole,

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:06.579
like, sort of second
half of your life,

00:09:06.579 --> 00:09:08.681
you knew that people wanted
something from you."

00:09:08.681 --> 00:09:10.783
And he seemed very good
at giving it.

00:09:10.783 --> 00:09:13.585
-I think he was one of
those rare people

00:09:13.585 --> 00:09:16.855
that didn't want to be the
richest man in the graveyard,

00:09:16.855 --> 00:09:22.294
but actually knew that we don't
get to take anything with us.

00:09:22.294 --> 00:09:25.297
You know, we are meant
to leave it all here.

00:09:25.297 --> 00:09:26.832
And I know Sondheim
was like that.

00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:29.702
And I know that Mike
was like that.

00:09:29.702 --> 00:09:31.470
And Rupert Murdoch doesn't seem
to be like that.

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:33.672
-Less, yeah.
-Yeah, slightly less.

00:09:33.672 --> 00:09:36.341
-If you had to order the three,
I think, like,

00:09:36.341 --> 00:09:39.811
maybe Mike and Sondheim
are, like, one and then, like --

00:09:39.811 --> 00:09:42.648
-Coming in hot at number three.
-Yeah, number three.

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:46.018
[ Laughter ]
Hey, last thing before you go,

00:09:46.018 --> 00:09:48.053
you know, there was a bit
of a controversy here

00:09:48.053 --> 00:09:50.022
on the show
when your "Spider-Man"

00:09:50.022 --> 00:09:53.325
costar Tom Holland
was here and he said something.

00:09:53.325 --> 00:09:56.028
-What happened?
-Well, he was talking about,

00:09:56.028 --> 00:09:58.564
you know, there were three
Spider-Men in that last movie.

00:09:58.564 --> 00:10:01.300
You were one of them, and he was
one of them, and one suit.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:02.568
He mentioned that one --

00:10:02.568 --> 00:10:04.536
Let's just take
a look real quick.

00:10:04.536 --> 00:10:07.740
-I'll give you a spoiler,
and I'm not gonna tell you who,

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.075
but one of us
has a fake ass in their suit.

00:10:10.075 --> 00:10:13.846
-Really?! Wow!
[ Laughter ]

00:10:13.846 --> 00:10:15.614
-You can figure out that
for yourselves.

00:10:15.614 --> 00:10:17.550
-Well.
[ Laughter ]

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.918
-He's just stirring the pot,
old Tom.

00:10:19.918 --> 00:10:21.420
He can't help himself.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.190
You know, there's an expression
that we have in entertainment,

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:26.525
which is,
we want to put bums on seats.

00:10:26.525 --> 00:10:29.294
You know, we want to get
the audience sat

00:10:29.294 --> 00:10:32.331
and paying their tickets,
and Tom is pretty great

00:10:32.331 --> 00:10:36.234
at creating subgenres of games
for you all,

00:10:36.234 --> 00:10:38.136
while you're in
the cinema going,

00:10:38.136 --> 00:10:40.305
"Well, that butt
doesn't look real.

00:10:40.305 --> 00:10:43.008
Toby's looks like
it might be real.

